An ingenious way to transform an economical, deeply beefy cut of chuck roast into a succulent steak dinner. The secret is an overnight balsamic vinegar marinade: the natural acids and enzymes break down the dense muscle fibers while olive oil, garlic, and cracked pepper season deep into the meat. Grilled hot, rested, and carved thin against the grain, it rivals expensive ribeye or flank steak.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/a39901485/grilled-chuck-roast-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

- ½ cup aged balsamic vinegar
- ¼ cup extra-virgin olive oil
- 4 cloves garlic, grated on a Microplane or finely minced
- 1 tablespoon kosher salt
- 1 tablespoon coarse freshly ground black pepper
- 1 boneless beef chuck roast (approx. 2 lbs, roughly 1 ½ inches thick)
- Flaky sea salt (for finishing)

## Instructions

1. **Whisk the Tenderizing Marinade:** In a large non-reactive baking dish or a 1-gallon heavy-duty freezer bag, whisk together the balsamic vinegar, olive oil, grated garlic, kosher salt, and coarse black pepper.
2. **Prep and Submerge the Meat:** Use a fork to pierce the chuck roast all over on both sides (about every inch). This allows the acidic balsamic marinade to penetrate deeply into the dense beef muscle fibers. Submerge the roast in the marinade, pressing out any excess air if using a bag, or rolling to coat if in a dish.
3. **Marinate Overnight:** Seal and refrigerate for a minimum of 12 hours, ideally 18 to 24 hours. Flip the roast halfway through to ensure both sides absorb the marinade equally.
4. **Fire Up the Grill:** Remove the marinated beef from the refrigerator 30 to 45 minutes before cooking so it comes toward room temperature. Preheat your outdoor grill (gas or charcoal) to medium-high direct heat (roughly 400°F to 450°F / 205°C to 230°C). Scrape the grill grates clean and lightly oil them.
5. **Grill and Turn Regularly:** Remove the roast from the marinade, shaking off excess liquid. Place directly over the hot grates. Grill for 20 to 25 minutes total, turning the roast every 5 minutes. Turning frequently prevents flare-ups from the rendered beef fat, avoids scorching the sugary balsamic exterior, and cooks the thick roast evenly from both sides.
6. **Check Internal Temperature:** Insert an instant-read meat thermometer into the thickest center part of the roast:
   - **Medium-Rare:** 135°F (57°C)
   - **Medium:** 140°F–145°F (60°C–63°C)
   *(Avoid cooking chuck steak beyond medium, as lean chuck can become tough without moisture).*
7. **Rest Thoroughly:** Transfer the grilled roast to a wooden cutting board with a juice groove. Tent loosely with aluminum foil and let rest for 10 full minutes. The internal temperature will carry over 5 degrees while the juices settle into the beef.
8. **Slice Thin Against the Grain:** Look closely at the surface of the roast to identify the grain direction. Carve the meat into thin slices (about ¼ inch thick) cutting perpendicular (against) the grain on a slight diagonal. Garnish with a sprinkle of flaky sea salt and serve warm.

## Notes

- **Why Slicing Against the Grain Matters:** Chuck meat has long, muscular grain fibers. Slicing thinly across the grain cuts those fibers into short pieces, guaranteeing an exceptionally tender chew.
- **Indoor Cooking Option:** Sear the roast in a screaming-hot cast iron grill pan or skillet over medium-high heat for 4 minutes per side, then transfer to a 375°F (190°C) oven for 12 to 15 minutes until internal temp reaches 135°F.
